29:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-16","children":[["$","section","01-four-resources",{"id":"01-four-resources","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"01"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Four resources, one method"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Strip away the frameworks and the cloud dashboards and a server is a very simple machine. It can compute (CPU), it can remember (memory), it can persist (disk I/O), and it can talk (network). That is the entire list. Every \"the app is slow\" ticket you will ever receive resolves to one of these four resources being the bottleneck, the point past which adding more work only adds more waiting. The skill is not knowing a hundred tools; it is knowing which resource is guilty and reaching for the one tool that proves it."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"To keep yourself honest, use a fixed methodology instead of poking around. The best known is Brendan Gregg’s USE method: for every resource, check three things: Utilization (what fraction of the time it is busy), Saturation (how much work is queued and waiting because it could not be served immediately), and Errors (outright failures). Utilization tells you it is busy; saturation tells you it is overwhelmed; errors tell you it is broken. A resource can be 100% utilized and perfectly healthy, but a resource with a growing saturation queue is always your problem."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Utilization"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The percentage of time the resource was busy over an interval. High is not automatically bad, a batch job should peg the CPU."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Saturation"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The extra work that could not be serviced immediately and had to queue: run-queue length, I/O wait, swap activity. This is the real pain signal."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Errors"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Hard failures: disk EIO, network drops and retransmits, failed allocations. Rare, but they mislead every latency graph when present."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Bottleneck"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The one resource whose saturation is limiting the whole system. Fixing anything else first is wasted effort."}]]}]]}],"$L2b","$L2c","$L2d"]}],"$L2e"]}]]}],"$L2f","$L30","$L31","$L32","$L33","$L34","$L35"]}]
2a:["$","$L36",null,{"prev":{"slug":"case-study-windows","title":"Case Study: Windows","kind":"$undefined","number":35},"next":{"slug":"os-for-databases","title":"Capstone: Operating Systems for Databases","kind":"$undefined","number":37}}]
37:I[76156,["2619","static/chunks/2619-04bc32f026a0d946.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","6238","static/chunks/app/operating-system/%5Bchapter%5D/page-6f5feff9906adfcd.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs"],"NoteImage"]
2b:["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"One habit underlies everything below: measure a rate over an interval, never a single instant. A tool that prints one snapshot and exits (like plain vmstat with no argument) shows you averages since boot, which are almost useless during an incident. Always give these tools an interval so they report per-second deltas of what is happening right now."}]
2c:["$","figure","4",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"The 60-second first look, a fast sweep across all four resources before you theorise."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"uptime                 # CPU: load average vs core count\nvmstat 1 5             # CPU + memory + swap, 5 one-second samples\nfree -h                # memory: used vs available vs cache\niostat -x 1 3          # disk: per-device %util and await\nss -s                  # network: socket/connection summary"}]}]]}]
2d:["$","div","5",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The frame",": "]}],"Four resources, three questions each. Do not open the profiler first. Walk CPU → memory → disk → network, find the saturated one, and only then dive. Ninety percent of incidents are solved by the first tool that shows a queue."]}]]}]
2e:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L37",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch36%2F01-four-resources.jpg?v=eb29c5009c8e82f19432e327cc860af7","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a 2x2 grid of the four machine resources: CPU, Memory, Disk I/O, Network, each cell listing its Linux tools (CPU: top/uptime/mpstat; Memory: free/vmstat; Disk: iostat/iotop; Network: sar/ss). Beside it a small USE-method card with three rows: Utilization (\"how busy, %\"), Saturation (\"queue / wait / swap\"), Errors (\"drops, retrans, EIO\"), and an arrow labelled \"walk each resource, ask all three\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
2f:["$","section","02-cpu-load-average",{"id":"02-cpu-load-average","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"02"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"CPU: load average and where the time goes"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Start with the cheapest signal on the machine. uptime prints the load average, three numbers for the last 1, 5, and 15 minutes. On Linux, load average is the average number of tasks that were either running on a CPU or runnable and waiting in the run queue (and, unlike other Unixes, it also counts tasks blocked in uninterruptible disk sleep). That last detail matters: a Linux load spike can mean \"CPU is busy\" or \"everything is stuck waiting on disk\", which is why load alone is a smoke alarm, not a diagnosis."}],["$","figure","1",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"Load average is meaningless until you divide by core count."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ uptime\n 02:14:07 up 9 days,  3:41,  2 users,  load average: 7.98, 6.42, 4.11\n\n$ nproc\n8"}]}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Read those together. This box has 8 cores and a 1-minute load of 7.98, almost exactly one runnable task per core, which is full but not overloaded. The trend (7.98 → 6.42 → 4.11 from recent to older) says the pressure is rising, not a spike that already passed. The rule of thumb: divide load by nproc. Around 1.0 per core is fully utilized; comfortably above 1.0 per core means tasks are queuing for the CPU and every one of them is now waiting longer than it runs, the run-queue saturation we studied with the scheduler."}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Load says how much; it does not say what kind. For that, break the CPU’s time into its states. top’s summary line and vmstat both split CPU time into user (%us, your application code), system (%sy, kernel work on your behalf, i.e. syscalls), iowait (%wa: CPU idle because it is waiting for disk to come back), and idle (%id). This one line often ends the investigation before it starts."}],["$","figure","4",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"vmstat 1, the run queue (r), plus the CPU breakdown on the right."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":"$L38"}]]}],"$L39","$L3a","$L3b"]}],"$L3c"]}]]}]
30:["$","section","03-reading-top",{"id":"03-reading-top","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"03"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Reading top and htop like a pro"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"top is the tool you will open first in almost every incident, so it is worth reading properly instead of just watching the numbers flicker. The top two lines are the summary from the previous section, load average and the %Cpu(s) breakdown, and the table below is one row per process. The art is knowing which four columns actually matter and ignoring the rest."}],["$","figure","1",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"top in batch mode (-b) so it prints once and is easy to paste into a ticket."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ top -b -n1 | head -n 12\ntop - 02:16:40 up 9 days,  load average: 8.01, 6.90, 4.55\nTasks: 214 total,   9 running, 205 sleeping,   0 stopped,   0 zombie\n%Cpu(s): 80.1 us, 14.9 sy,  0.0 ni,  3.0 id,  1.8 wa,  0.0 hi,  0.2 si\nMiB Mem :  15884.0 total,    500.3 free,  11210.6 used,   4173.1 buff/cache\nMiB Swap:   2048.0 total,   1998.0 free,     50.0 used.   3980.2 avail Mem\n\n    PID USER      PR  NI    VIRT    RES    SHR S  %CPU  %MEM     TIME+ COMMAND\n  30412 app       20   0 12.4g   9.8g   12m R 512.0  63.1  418:22.10 java\n   1188 postgres  20   0 1024m  212m  200m S   8.3   1.3   64:12.44 postgres\n   9930 app       20   0  742m   88m   14m S   2.0   0.5    2:41.09 node\n    884 root      20   0  120m   14m    8m S   0.3   0.1   12:03.77 systemd"}]}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The culprit is obvious once you know the columns. That java process shows %CPU of 512.0, and no, that is not a bug. top reports CPU as a percentage of a single core, so on this 8-core box the maximum is 800%, and 512% means it is actively using about five cores. Its RES is 9.8g and %MEM is 63.1, so it is also holding two-thirds of physical RAM. One process is both the CPU hog and the memory hog; that is where the investigation goes."}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The two memory columns confuse people forever, so pin them down now. VIRT is the process’s virtual size (VSZ), the total address space it has mapped, including memory-mapped files, shared libraries, and pages it reserved but never touched. It is mostly promises. RES is the resident set size (RSS), the physical RAM actually backing the process right now. When you are hunting a memory problem, RES is the number that matters; a huge VIRT with a modest RES is completely normal and not a leak. This is the same VSZ-versus-RSS distinction from the virtual-memory chapter, now on a live screen."}],"$L3d","$L3e","$L3f","$L40"]}],"$L41"]}]]}]
31:["$","section","04-memory-really-full",{"id":"04-memory-really-full","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"04"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Memory: is it really full?"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"More false alarms are raised over memory than any other resource, almost always because someone read the wrong number. A healthy, hard-working Linux box will report almost all of its RAM as \"used\" and that is exactly how it is supposed to look. The reason is the page cache: the kernel keeps recently read file data in otherwise-free RAM so the next read is instant, as we saw in the file-I/O chapter. That memory is being used, but it is instantly reclaimable, the moment a process needs real memory, the kernel drops cache pages to give it room. Cache is not consumption."}],["$","figure","1",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"free -h, read the \"available\" column, not \"used\"."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ free -h\n               total        used        free      shared  buff/cache   available\nMem:            15Gi        10Gi       480Mi       120Mi       4.6Gi       4.9Gi\nSwap:          2.0Gi        50Mi       2.0Gi"}]}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Walk this line honestly. free shows only 480 MiB truly free, and a panicky reading stops there. But 4.6 GiB is buff/cache, reclaimable file cache, and the column that tells the truth is available: 4.9 GiB. That is the kernel’s own estimate of how much memory a new process could get right now without swapping, because it would evict cache if needed. On any modern kernel, available is the number you alert on. This machine is fine: it has ~5 GiB of headroom dressed up as \"used\"."}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"So when is memory genuinely a problem? When there is nothing left to reclaim and the kernel is forced to start swapping, pushing anonymous (non-file) pages out to disk to make room, then reading them back when they are needed again. A little swap sitting used is harmless (idle pages parked on disk). The danger is sustained swap traffic, and vmstat’s si (swap-in) and so (swap-out) columns are the cleanest signal on the machine."}],["$","figure","4",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"A thrashing machine seen through vmstat, si/so are pouring, iowait is high, throughput has collapsed."}],"$L42"]}],"$L43"]}],"$L44","$L45","$L46","$L47","$L48"]}],"$L49"]}]]}]
32:["$","section","05-disk-io",{"id":"05-disk-io","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"05"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Disk I/O: throughput is not latency"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"When the CPU is idle but the system is still slow, and %wa is high, the disk is the prime suspect. The tool that settles it is iostat -x, which prints extended per-device statistics. Run it with an interval so it reports live rates, and discard the very first sample, that one is the average since boot and will lie to you."}],["$","figure","1",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"iostat -x 1, extended stats; watch r/s, w/s, await, and %util."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ iostat -x 1 2\nDevice   r/s    w/s   rkB/s   wkB/s  r_await w_await  aqu-sz  %util\nnvme0n1  12.0   40.0  480.0  2100.0    0.42    0.55    0.03   3.10\n\n# ...second sample, under a heavy query load:\nDevice   r/s    w/s   rkB/s   wkB/s  r_await w_await  aqu-sz  %util\nsdb     980.0   14.0 15680.0   224.0   89.30   12.40   88.20  99.60"}]}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The two devices tell opposite stories and teach the whole lesson. nvme0n1 is doing modest I/O at 0.42 ms per read and 3% utilization, effectively idle. sdb is the problem: %util is 99.6%, meaning the device was busy essentially the entire interval, r_await is 89.3 ms (each read takes nearly a tenth of a second to come back), and aqu-sz, the average queue depth, is 88, meaning ~88 requests were stacked up waiting at any moment. That queue is disk saturation in the USE sense, and the 89 ms await is what your users experience as a slow page."}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This is the single most important distinction in storage performance: throughput is not latency. Throughput is how many bytes per second the device moves (rkB/s, wkB/s); latency is how long any one request takes to complete (await). A disk can post excellent throughput while every individual request is agonisingly slow, because throughput rewards big sequential transfers while latency is what a single small random read feels. Users and databases live in latency. A dashboard that only graphs MB/s will happily show green while await quietly ruins you."}],["$","p","4",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The other trap is r/s and w/s, reads and writes per second, i.e. IOPS. These are operations, not bytes. A workload doing 980 tiny 16 KB random reads per second can saturate a device that would barely notice the same bytes delivered as a few large sequential streams, because each seek and each request has fixed overhead. When await climbs with high IOPS but low kB/s, you are looking at a random-I/O pattern, the classic profile of an under-indexed database doing scattered lookups."}],"$L4a","$L4b","$L4c","$L4d"]}],"$L4e"]}]]}]
33:["$","section","06-context-switches",{"id":"06-context-switches","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"06"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Context switches, interrupts & too many threads"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Sometimes CPU, memory, and disk all look fine and the system is still sluggish. The hidden tax is often context switching, the cost of the scheduler saving one task’s state and loading another’s, which we met in the scheduling chapter. Each switch is cheap, but at tens of thousands per second the machine spends its time swapping tasks in and out rather than running them, and useful work quietly evaporates into overhead."}],["$","figure","1",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"vmstat again, this time watch the cs (context switches) and in (interrupts) columns."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ vmstat 1 5\nprocs -----------memory----------  ---system---- ----cpu----\n r  b   swpd   free   cache        in      cs    us sy id wa\n 6  0      0 480112  3.4g      12010  188540    22 61 15  2\n 7  0      0 479980  3.4g      12240  192880    20 64 14  2\n 5  0      0 480560  3.4g      11980  190220    23 62 13  2"}]}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The tell here is not the CPU being busy but where it is busy. cs is ~190,000 context switches per second, and %sy (system/kernel time) is over 60% while %us (your actual code) is only ~20%. The machine is burning most of its CPU inside the kernel just scheduling, not running your application. High system time with a high context-switch rate is the fingerprint of either lock contention or simply too many threads fighting over too few cores."}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"To tell those two apart, split the switches by cause with pidstat -w. A context switch is voluntary (cswch/s) when a task gives up the CPU on its own because it must wait, blocked on a lock, a condition variable, or an I/O it cannot complete yet. It is involuntary (nvcswch/s) when the scheduler forcibly preempts it because its time slice expired and other runnable tasks are queued. The ratio between them points straight at the cause."}],["$","figure","4",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"pidstat -w -p <pid> 1, voluntary vs involuntary switches per thread/process."}],"$L4f"]}],"$L50"]}],"$L51","$L52","$L53","$L54"]}],"$L55"]}]]}]
34:["$","section","07-tracing-profiling",{"id":"07-tracing-profiling","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"07"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Tracing and profiling: strace, perf, flame graphs"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The tools so far told you which resource is saturated. Tracing and profiling tell you why, down to the syscall and the function. They answer two different questions, and reaching for the wrong one wastes an incident. Ask \"what is my process asking the kernel to do, and where is it stuck?\" and you want strace. Ask \"which of my functions is actually burning the CPU?\" and you want perf."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"strace lists every system call a process makes with its arguments, return value, and errno, the boundary from the syscalls chapter, made visible on a live process. Its killer feature under pressure is -c, which does not print the flood of individual calls but a summary table of where syscall time and count went. Attach it to a running process with -p, let it sample for a few seconds, then Ctrl-C."}],["$","figure","2",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"strace -c on a live process, where is it spending kernel time?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ sudo strace -c -p 9930\nstrace: Process 9930 attached\n^Cstrace: Process 9930 detached\n% time     seconds  usecs/call     calls    errors syscall\n------ ----------- ----------- --------- --------- ----------------\n 74.10    2.104880          51     41200           read\n 18.30    0.519900          25     20600           write\n  6.90    0.196010          95      2060      2060 futex\n  0.70    0.019880           9      2100           epoll_wait\n------ ----------- ----------- --------- --------- ----------------\n100.00    2.840670                 65960      2060 total"}]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Read the summary as a story. This process made 41,200 read calls in a few seconds, it is I/O-syscall-bound, hammering read, and the fix is almost certainly to read in bigger chunks so it crosses the boundary far fewer times, the exact batching lesson from the syscalls chapter. The errors column also flags 2,060 failing futex calls, a hint of lock churn worth correlating with the context-switch reading. One caveat that matters in production: strace uses ptrace and can dramatically slow the traced process, so trace briefly and never leave it attached to a busy service."}],["$","p","4",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"When the problem is CPU-bound, high %us, your own code eating cores, strace is useless because the process is not making syscalls; it is computing. That is perf’s job. perf is a sampling profiler: many times a second it interrupts the CPU and records the current call stack, so the functions that appear most often are, by definition, where the time goes. perf top gives you a live, htop-like view of the hottest functions across the whole system."}],"$L56","$L57","$L58","$L59","$L5a","$L5b"]}],"$L5c"]}]]}]
35:["$","section","08-debugging-playbook",{"id":"08-debugging-playbook","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"08"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"The debugging playbook"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Put it all together and performance debugging becomes a two-question funnel you can run cold, half-asleep, during an incident. First: which of the four resources is saturated? Walk them in order with the 60-second sweep from the first section and stop at the one with a queue. Second: which layer owns the problem, your application code, the syscalls it makes, the kernel, or the device underneath? The first question picks the resource; the second picks the tool. Everything in this chapter slots into that grid."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The table below is the playbook itself, symptom to tool to cause. It is worth committing to muscle memory, because the difference between a ten-minute incident and a three-hour one is usually just knowing which tool to open first instead of guessing."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"App slow, load high, %us high"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"CPU-bound in your own code. Tool: top (confirm), then perf top / flame graph. Cause: a hot function, the recompute-style hotspot."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Load high, %wa high, CPU idle"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"I/O-bound. Tool: iostat -x (await, %util), then iotop for the process. Cause: disk saturation, random I/O, or an unindexed query."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Slow, RAM \"full\", swap busy"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Thrashing. Tool: free -h (read available), vmstat si/so. Cause: working set exceeds RAM, add memory or shrink footprint."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Process vanished, no crash log"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"OOM kill. Tool: dmesg -T | grep -i kill. Cause: memory exhausted; the kernel sacrificed the biggest process to survive."}]]}],["$","div","4",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Latency spikes, %sy + cs high"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Scheduling overhead. Tool: pidstat -w. Cause: lock contention (voluntary switches) or too many threads (involuntary switches)."}]]}],"$L5d"]}],"$L5e","$L5f","$L60","$L61"]}],"$L62"]}]]}]
38:["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ vmstat 1 5\nprocs -----------memory----------  ---swap-- ----cpu----\n r  b   swpd   free   buff  cache   si   so   us sy id wa\n 9  0      0 512344  88120 3421556    0    0   74 18  6  2\n11  0      0 509880  88120 3421560    0    0   79 15  4  2\n10  1      0 508112  88120 3421572    0    0   81 14  3  2"}]
39:["$","p","5",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The r column is the number of tasks on the run queue, here 9 to 11 against 8 cores, confirming CPU saturation directly. The CPU is ~80% user, ~15% system, near-zero iowait: this is a genuinely CPU-bound, compute-heavy workload, and the fix lives in your code or in adding cores, not in the disk. Contrast that with the opposite reading, low %us, low %sy, but high %wa and a b (blocked) column that keeps climbing, which is not a CPU problem at all but a disk problem masquerading as high load. Same load number, completely different fix."}]
3a:["$","dl","6",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Load average"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Average count of running + runnable (+ uninterruptible-sleep) tasks over 1/5/15 min. Divide by core count to interpret."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Run queue (r in vmstat)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Tasks ready to run right now but waiting for a CPU. Consistently above core count = CPU saturation."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"%us / %sy"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Time in your user code vs time in the kernel serving your syscalls. High %sy points at syscall or context-switch overhead."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"%wa (iowait)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"CPU sitting idle because it is blocked waiting on I/O. High iowait means the bottleneck is the disk, not the CPU."}]]}]]}]
3b:["$","div","7",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The distinction that saves hours",": "]}],"High load + high %us = CPU-bound; profile the code. High load + high %wa = I/O-bound; the CPU is idle, waiting. If you \"add more CPU\" to an iowait problem you will spend money and change nothing."]}]]}]
3c:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L37",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch36%2F02-cpu-load-average.jpg?v=1ca3f62140f760fccbc60ae01d1f286b","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: top of the page shows \"uptime\" output with the three load-average numbers circled and an arrow to a core-count ruler (\"8 cores → load 8 = fully used, load 16 = 2x overloaded\"). Below, a run queue drawn as tasks lined up behind one CPU (callback to scheduler chapter). At the bottom, a bar split into %us / %sy / %wa / %id with each slice annotated: user code, kernel, waiting on disk, idle."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
3d:["$","dl","4",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"VIRT (VSZ)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Total virtual address space mapped by the process, includes untouched reservations and shared libraries. Big by design; rarely the problem."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"RES (RSS)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Physical RAM the process currently occupies. The real memory-pressure number. Shared pages are counted in each sharer, so RES can over-count across processes."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"%CPU"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Percent of one core. Can exceed 100% for multithreaded processes; ceiling is 100% × number of cores."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"S (state)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"R = running/runnable, S = interruptible sleep (normal idle wait), D = uninterruptible sleep (stuck in I/O, watch this), Z = zombie (dead, unreaped)."}]]}]]}]
3e:["$","p","5",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The state column is the quiet hero. A process parked in S is just waiting politely for work and costs nothing. A process stuck in D, uninterruptible sleep, is blocked inside a kernel I/O call and cannot even be killed with a normal signal; a pile of D-state processes is the classic signature of a saturated or hung disk (or an unresponsive NFS mount). And a Z, a zombie, is a finished child whose parent never called wait to reap it, harmless in ones and twos, but a growing count means a buggy parent, exactly the reaping problem from the process-lifecycle chapter."}]
3f:["$","p","6",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Two moves turn top from a dashboard into an investigation. Inside interactive top, press M to sort by memory or P to sort by CPU so the worst offender floats to the top instantly. And press H (or run top -H -p <pid>) to expand a process into its individual threads, indispensable when one thread of a many-threaded server is spinning at 100% while the process as a whole looks merely busy. htop gives you the same data with per-core bars and easier scrolling, but the columns mean exactly the same thing."}]
40:["$","div","7",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Pin the culprit",": "]}],"Sort by the resource you suspect (P for CPU, M for memory), read RES not VIRT for memory, and check the state column for a wall of D or a creep of Z. One annotated top screen resolves more incidents than any dashboard."]}]]}]
41:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L37",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch36%2F03-reading-top.jpg?v=d3e6d10a4a17b40f94883f3824ef7747","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a full \"top\" screen drawn by hand and annotated, the load line and %Cpu(s) line at the top boxed, then the process table with arrows to key columns: VIRT (VSZ) labelled \"address space, mostly promises\", RES (RSS) labelled \"real physical RAM, watch this one\", %CPU labelled \"can exceed 100% = multiple cores\", S (state) with a legend R/S/D/Z, and one runaway row highlighted as \"the culprit\". A side note: press H for per-thread, M to sort by memory, P by CPU."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
42:["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]
43:["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ vmstat 1 5\nprocs -----------memory----------  ---swap--  ----cpu----\n r  b   swpd   free   buff  cache    si   so   us sy id wa\n 1 6  1980312  38112   1024  40220  4820 5120    5  9  2 84\n 2 7  1998640  35880   1020  39880  5360 5980    4  8  1 87\n 1 8  2011220  33110   1016  38990  6110 6480    6 10  1 83"}]}]
44:["$","p","5",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This is thrashing, and it is unmistakable. swpd (total swap in use) is climbing, si and so are in the thousands of KB per second every second, iowait (wa) is 84–87% because the CPU is doing nothing but waiting for the swap disk, and the run-queue is small while the blocked (b) column grows. The machine is spending its life shuttling pages between RAM and disk instead of doing work, precisely the page-replacement failure mode from the virtual-memory chapter, where the working set no longer fits in RAM. Throughput falls off a cliff and latency goes vertical."}]
45:["$","p","6",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The other end of memory pressure is abrupt rather than gradual: the OOM killer. When memory is exhausted and swap cannot save it, the kernel picks a process (roughly, the one with the largest badness score) and kills it to survive. You will not see it in top, the process is simply gone, so you must look in the kernel log."}]
46:["$","figure","7",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"Confirming an OOM kill after a process mysteriously vanished."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ dmesg -T | grep -i -A1 \"killed process\"\n[Tue Jul  8 02:41:12 2026] Out of memory: Killed process 30412 (java)\n  total-vm:13010224kB, anon-rss:10297540kB, file-rss:0kB, oom_score_adj:0"}]}]]}]
47:["$","dl","8",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"buff/cache"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"File data and metadata the kernel caches in spare RAM. Reclaimable on demand, counts as \"used\" but is not a shortage."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"available"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The kernel’s estimate of memory obtainable now without swapping (free + reclaimable cache). The real free-memory metric."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"si / so"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Swap-in and swap-out rate in vmstat. Sustained non-zero values are the definitive thrashing signal."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"OOM killer"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Kernel last resort that terminates a process to reclaim memory when both RAM and swap are exhausted. Logged in dmesg."}]]}]]}]
48:["$","div","9",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The rule",": "]}],"Never diagnose memory from \"free\"; diagnose it from \"available\" and from vmstat si/so. Full RAM with zero swap traffic is a healthy cache. A trickle of sustained swap-in/out is your latency killer."]}]]}]
49:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L37",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch36%2F04-memory-really-full.jpg?v=c645a1c1faafefa71cc3e411b78d9ff5","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: \"free -h\" output drawn as a horizontal bar of total RAM split into used, buff/cache, and free, with a big arrow from buff/cache down to the label \"the page cache, reclaimable, NOT really used\". A separate boxed \"available\" figure is circled as \"the number that actually matters\". Below, a vmstat strip with the si and so columns circled in red and labelled \"swap in / swap out > 0 sustained = THRASHING (callback: page replacement)\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
4a:["$","p","5",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Once iostat proves the disk is saturated, one question remains: who is doing it? iotop answers directly, ranking processes by their actual read and write rates, the disk-world equivalent of top."}]
4b:["$","figure","6",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"iotop -o, only show processes actually doing I/O right now."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ sudo iotop -b -o -n1\nTotal DISK READ: 15.30 M/s | Total DISK WRITE: 0.22 M/s\n  TID  PRIO  USER   DISK READ  DISK WRITE  COMMAND\n 1188  be/4  postgres 14.90 M/s   0.00 B/s  postgres: parallel worker ...\n 1190  be/4  postgres  0.40 M/s   0.20 B/s  postgres: walwriter"}]}]]}]
4c:["$","dl","7",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"r/s, w/s (IOPS)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Read and write operations per second. Counts requests, not bytes, many small random ops hurt far more than a few large ones."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"await"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Average time each I/O takes to complete, in milliseconds, including queue time. The latency your application and users actually feel."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"%util"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Fraction of the interval the device was busy. Near 100% means saturation on a single spindle (less definitive on SSDs/arrays that serve in parallel)."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"aqu-sz"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Average number of requests queued at the device. A deep queue with high await is the definition of a disk bottleneck."}]]}]]}]
4d:["$","div","8",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Storage truth",": "]}],"Green MB/s with red await is still a fire. Alert on latency (await) and queue depth, not throughput. Then let iotop name the process, usually a database, a log flush, or a backup job running at the worst possible time."]}]]}]
4e:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L37",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch36%2F05-disk-io.jpg?v=724a85888aa183249fdd5e724092ed2f","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: \"iostat -x\" table drawn out with three columns circled and annotated, r/s + w/s labelled \"IOPS (operations, not bytes)\", await labelled \"latency per I/O in ms, the number users feel\", and %util labelled \"how busy the device is; ~100% = saturated\". A small graph contrasts two devices: one at high MB/s but low await (\"fast, healthy\"), one at low MB/s but 90ms await and 99% util (\"saturated, the bottleneck\"). Corner note: iotop shows which process is doing the I/O."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
4f:["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]
50:["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ pidstat -w -p 30412 1 3\n#      Time   UID     PID   cswch/s  nvcswch/s  Command\n   02:31:10  1001   30412  48210.0     120.0    java\n   02:31:11  1001   30412  47980.0     138.0    java\n   02:31:12  1001   30412  49110.0     115.0    java"}]}]
51:["$","p","5",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This process is doing ~48,000 voluntary switches per second and almost no involuntary ones. That lopsided ratio is the signature of lock contention: threads keep acquiring a lock, doing a sliver of work, blocking, and handing the CPU away, over and over. The threads are not being preempted; they are constantly putting themselves to sleep waiting on each other, exactly the synchronization bottleneck from the concurrency chapters. The opposite reading, high nvcswch/s (involuntary), means you simply have more runnable threads than cores and the scheduler is time-slicing frantically; the fix is fewer threads, not faster ones. A thread pool sized to 500 on an 8-core box does not run 500 things at once; it just pays 500 threads’ worth of switching overhead."}]
52:["$","p","6",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The in column is the companion signal: hardware and timer interrupts per second. A network-heavy box under a packet flood, or a misbehaving device, will show interrupts spiking, and mpstat -P ALL will reveal the kernel time concentrated in soft-interrupt handling (%soft) on specific cores. It is the same underlying story, the CPU is busy with kernel bookkeeping rather than your code, just driven by devices instead of by threads."}]
53:["$","dl","7",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Context switch (cs)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Scheduler saving one task and restoring another. Necessary, but tens of thousands per second is pure overhead eating your throughput."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Voluntary (cswch/s)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Task yields the CPU itself because it must wait, on a lock, condition variable, or I/O. A high rate points at contention or blocking."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Involuntary (nvcswch/s)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Scheduler preempts the task because its slice ended and others are queued. A high rate points at too many runnable threads for the cores."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Interrupts (in)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Hardware/timer signals per second. Spikes point at network or device load; inspect per-core with mpstat -P ALL."}]]}]]}]
54:["$","div","8",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Read the ratio",": "]}],"High %sy + high cs means the kernel, not your code, owns the CPU. Voluntary-heavy → lock contention (reduce the critical section). Involuntary-heavy → oversubscribed threads (shrink the pool toward the core count)."]}]]}]
55:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L37",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch36%2F06-context-switches.jpg?v=e2c278c21c64bac79fa5d71faf2dc3ca","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: vmstat strip with the \"cs\" and \"in\" columns circled and labelled \"context switches / sec\" and \"interrupts / sec\". Below, two thread timelines, one making steady progress, one chopped into tiny slivers by constant switching, labelled \"thrashing on the CPU\". A \"pidstat -w\" mini-table splits cswch/s (voluntary, waiting on lock/IO) from nvcswch/s (involuntary, quantum expired) with arrows: \"lots of voluntary = lock contention\", \"lots of involuntary = too many runnable threads\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
56:["$","figure","5",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"perf top, live ranking of the hottest functions (kernel [k] and user [.])."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ sudo perf top\nSamples: 480K of event \"cpu-clock\", Event count: 121000000000\nOverhead  Shared Object        Symbol\n  38.42%  app.jar (java)       [.] com.acme.pricing.recompute\n  15.10%  libc-2.31.so         [.] __memmove_avx_unaligned\n   9.77%  [kernel]             [k] copy_user_enhanced_fast_string\n   4.31%  app.jar (java)       [.] java.util.HashMap.resize"}]}]]}]
57:["$","p","6",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The answer is right there: one application method, recompute, is 38% of all CPU samples on the machine. That is your hotspot. For a durable artifact, and for anything with a deep call stack, record instead of watch, then fold the samples into a flame graph. perf record captures stacks over an interval; the flame-graph tooling turns them into an SVG where the x-axis is the proportion of samples (wider = more CPU) and the y-axis is stack depth. You do not read a flame graph top-to-bottom; you scan for the widest plateaus, because a wide frame means the CPU sat in that function (and its children) for a large share of the samples."}]
58:["$","figure","7",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"Record stacks, then render a flame graph."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"# Sample the whole system at 99 Hz for 30 seconds\nsudo perf record -F 99 -a -g -- sleep 30\n\n# Fold and render (Brendan Gregg’s FlameGraph scripts)\nperf script | ./stackcollapse-perf.pl | ./flamegraph.pl > cpu.svg"}]}]]}]
59:["$","p","8",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"There is also ltrace, the same idea as strace but for library calls rather than syscalls, handy when the time is going into a userspace library (say, repeated regex compilation) that never crosses into the kernel. And the modern frontier is eBPF, which safely runs tiny sandboxed programs inside the kernel to measure things with far less overhead than ptrace-based strace, safe enough to run on production. The bcc/bpftrace toolkit gives you targeted one-liners: execsnoop traces every process that starts, opensnoop every file opened, biolatency draws a histogram of disk-I/O latency, and tcpconnect logs outbound connections. When strace is too heavy and perf too broad, an eBPF one-liner answers a precise question cheaply."}]
5a:["$","dl","9",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"strace"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Traces system calls (the kernel boundary). Use -c for a time/count summary. High overhead, trace briefly, never leave attached in production."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"perf"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"CPU sampling profiler. perf top for a live hotspot view; perf record + flame graph to find which function burns the CPU."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Flame graph"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Stacks rolled up into a chart: width = share of CPU samples, height = stack depth. Hunt the widest frames, that is where to optimise."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"eBPF / bcc"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Sandboxed in-kernel programs for low-overhead, production-safe tracing. execsnoop, opensnoop, biolatency answer precise questions cheaply."}]]}]]}]
5b:["$","div","10",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Pick the right lens",": "]}],"Stuck or syscall-heavy (high %sy, or blocked/waiting) → strace to see the calls. CPU-burning (high %us) → perf + flame graph to find the hot function. Production and need it cheap → an eBPF one-liner."]}]]}]
5c:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L37",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch36%2F07-tracing-profiling.jpg?v=c0e9db957cb037b287b71a33b8fe47cc","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a decision tree at the top: \"app slow?\" branching to \"which syscalls / why blocked? → strace\" and \"which code is hot on CPU? → perf\". Below, a hand-drawn flame graph: a wide base frame with stacked narrower frames above, the widest top plateau shaded and labelled \"hottest = most samples = fix here\". A side box lists modern eBPF/bcc one-liners (execsnoop, biolatency, opensnoop) tagged \"low overhead, production-safe\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
5d:["$","div","5",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Fails with no message"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Unknown syscall error. Tool: strace -p to catch the failing call and its errno. Cause: a bad path, permission, or exhausted resource (e.g. fd leak)."}]]}]
5e:["$","figure","3",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"The incident checklist, run top to bottom, stop at the first queue you find."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"uptime            # 1. load vs cores, is anything saturated at all?\nvmstat 1 5        # 2. run queue (r), swap (si/so), switches (cs), cpu split\ntop -b -n1        # 3. the culprit process: %CPU, RES, state\nfree -h           # 4. memory: available, not used\niostat -x 1 3     # 5. disk: await + %util if %wa was high\ndmesg -T | tail   # 6. errors: OOM kills, device resets, kernel warnings"}]}]]}]
5f:["$","p","4",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Notice how every entry in that table is an operating-system concept you already learned, now wearing an incident’s clothes. \"High load\" is the run queue from the scheduling chapter. \"Thrashing\" is page replacement failing when the working set outgrows RAM. \"%wa\" is the I/O-wait state from the process-lifecycle chapter. \"Voluntary context switches\" are threads blocking on the locks from the concurrency chapters. \"OOM\" is virtual memory finally running out of physical backing. You are not learning a separate discipline called \"performance\"; you are applying the OS model to a live machine."}]
60:["$","p","5",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"That is the real payoff of this whole course for a working engineer. The invisible layer under your code is now legible: you can look at a struggling server and reason about it, name the resource, name the layer, reach for the one tool that proves it, and fix the actual bottleneck instead of restarting things and hoping. \"It just works\" has become \"I know exactly what the OS is doing, and I can prove it\"."}]
61:["$","div","6",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["What is next",": "]}],"One system pulls every thread of this course together, a database. In the capstone we watch Postgres lean on the scheduler, the page cache, fsync, locks, and virtual memory all at once, and use exactly this playbook to reason about a real engine under load. On to the database capstone."]}]]}]
62:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L37",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch36%2F08-debugging-playbook.jpg?v=0117d522b34c2943a456c2f3b346c97a","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a large symptom→tool→cause table with rows: \"app slow, high load, high %us → perf/top → CPU-bound code\"; \"high load, high %wa → iostat/iotop → disk saturated\"; \"process vanished → dmesg → OOM kill\"; \"latency spikes, high cs → pidstat -w → lock contention / too many threads\"; \"slow but idle CPU → free/vmstat si-so → thrashing/swap\". Beside it, a decision funnel: \"which resource? (CPU/mem/disk/net) → which layer? (app / syscall / kernel / device)\". A footer arrow points to \"→ ch37 Database capstone\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
1d:[["$","meta","0",{"charSet":"utf-8"}],["$","meta","1",{"name":"viewport","content":"width=device-width, initial-scale=1"}]]
